| Feature               | `CrudRepository` | `JpaRepository` (extends CrudRepository)        |
| --------------------- | ---------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |
| Basic CRUD operations | ✅ Yes            | ✅ Yes                                           |
| Pagination & Sorting  | ❌ No             | ✅ Yes (`findAll(Pageable)` etc.)                |
| JPA-specific features | ❌ No             | ✅ Yes (`flush()`, `saveAndFlush()` etc.)        |
| Default use case      | Simple CRUD apps | Most real-world JPA use cases                   |
| Extends               | —                | `PagingAndSortingRepository` → `CrudRepository` |

✔️ Use CrudRepository when:
    You only need basic Create, Read, Update, Delete
    No pagination, sorting, or JPA-specific operations needed
    Lightweight/simple apps or quick demos

✔️ Use JpaRepository when:
    You want pagination, sorting, or advanced JPA support
    Need methods like findAll(Pageable), flush(), saveAndFlush(), etc.
    Working with Spring Data JPA in a production-grade application

Why is a no-arg constructor needed in JPA?
    JPA (Java Persistence API) requires that every entity class has a public or protected no-argument constructor, for the following reasons:
    When JPA (via Hibernate or any ORM) creates objects while reading from the database, it uses reflection.
    It does not use your custom constructors — instead, it:
    Instantiates the entity using the no-arg constructor Then sets fields one-by-one using reflection

