Applications often consist of similar but different object models, where the data in two models may be similar but
the structure and concerns of the models are different. Object mapping makes it easy to convert one model to another,
allowing separate models to remain segregated, so model mapper used.

ModelMapper is just a tool to help you automatically convert between:
Entity ⟷ DTO

Let’s say you’re converting manually:
    UserResponseDTO dto = new UserResponseDTO();
    dto.setId(user.getId());
    dto.setName(user.getName());
    dto.setEmail(user.getEmail());

🔁 With ModelMapper:
    ModelMapper mapper = new ModelMapper();
    UserResponseDTO dto = mapper.map(user, UserResponseDTO.class);

🔄 Think of ModelMapper as a 2-way converter:
    | Use Case                          | Mapping                |
    | --------------------------------- | ---------------------- |
    | **Create/Update (POST/PUT)**      | `RequestDTO ➜ Entity`  |
    | **Read (GET) or Return Response** | `Entity ➜ ResponseDTO` |

📘 @PathVariable vs @RequestParam – Use Case Comparison
   | 🎯 **Use Case**                                | ✅ **Use**      | 📘 **When to Use**                                                   | 🔍 **Example URL**                       |
   | ---------------------------------------------- | --------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------- |
   | Fetch a specific resource by ID                | `@PathVariable` | When identifying a single item (RESTful resource)                    | `/categories/5`                          |
   | Filter/search data based on criteria           | `@RequestParam` | When applying filters like name, status, etc.                        | `/categories?name=Electronics`           |
   | Pass optional values                           | `@RequestParam` | When parameters are not mandatory                                    | `/products?discounted=true`              |
   | RESTful endpoint design                        | `@PathVariable` | When URL structure should represent hierarchy/resources clearly      | `/users/10/orders/7`                     |
   | Implement pagination                           | `@RequestParam` | When passing page number, size, sort, etc.                           | `/products?page=1&size=10`               |
   | Sorting or ordering results                    | `@RequestParam` | When user can request sorting dynamically                            | `/products?sortBy=price&order=desc`      |
   | Handling fixed structured URL paths            | `@PathVariable` | When the URL pattern is consistent and mapped to data model directly | `/orders/1234/status`                    |
   | Handling dynamic and optional input parameters | `@RequestParam` | When you want to customize what to include in the query              | `/orders/search?status=shipped&type=COD` |

✅ Why do we use both Pageable and Page<T>?
    | Term       | Purpose                                                                                  |
    | ---------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
    | `Pageable` | Used to **pass input** (page number, size, sort) to repository methods                   |
    | `Page<T>`  | Used to **get output** — the actual **content + metadata** like total pages, total items |
Pageable pageable = PageRequest.of(pageNumber, pageSize); // You pass this IN
Page<Category> page = categoryRepository.findAll(pageable); // You get this OUT

